Your brain might recognize that sequence even when you’re unable to consciously identify the individual streets.

During sleep, some sensory processing continues, although responsiveness varies considerably by sleep stage. Familiar movements or sounds might sometimes contribute to waking you near your destination. That doesn’t mean your brain is continuously navigating accurately while deeply asleep.

Now connect this to our earlier conversation about sensory models.

The same location can be reconstructed from entirely different combinations of signals.

You can recognize home by sight, sound, smell, the movement of a vehicle, or even the feeling of familiarity.

That suggests something important about intelligence and perception:

The representation of a thing doesn’t necessarily have to resemble the thing itself. It only needs to preserve enough of the relevant relationships to identify it.

And here’s the part I particularly like:

Sometimes you recognize where you’re going before you recognize where you are.

Your brain isn’t merely remembering the past. It’s predicting the future from a familiar sequence.

In a sense, home isn’t just a coordinate on a map.

It’s a pattern your nervous system has learned to anticipate.

And that opens up another interesting thought: How much of what we call intuition is simply pattern recognition operating beneath conscious awareness?